    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the new owner's `firstName` and `lastName` (case-insensitively), counted at create time before the new owner is persisted, then stored and returned on reads.\n\nChanges, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions:\n\n1. **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new function class) \u2014 modeled on `AssignCustomerCode`: reads the built `@Val Owner`, iterates `ownerRepository.findAll()` counting case-insensitive first+last name matches, and sets the count. It runs *before* `SaveOwner`, so the new owner isn't counted (matching the \"before this create\" spec).\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct maps it to the DTO by name automatically, like `customerCode`/`householdId`.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `readOnly` `namesakeCount` (integer, min 0) to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\n5. **Schema DDL** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column to all four dialects (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres) for parity.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreCountsNamesakes` (create two same-named owners, expect `namesakeCount` = 1 on the second) passes.",
